1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,925
The Hotel de l'Europe, Singapore's
Grand Hotel Before the Raffles

2
00:00:04,925 --> 00:00:09,348
In 1889, a young Rudyard Kipling
passed through Singapore and

3
00:00:09,428 --> 00:00:14,958
left its hotels with a piece of advice
that travellers repeated for decades:

4
00:00:15,038 --> 00:00:18,950
"Feed at Raffles and sleep
at the Hotel de l'Europe."

5
00:00:18,950 --> 00:00:22,162
The Raffles Hotel's managers
liked the first half

6
00:00:22,242 --> 00:00:26,461
so much that they quietly borrowed
it for their own advertising,

7
00:00:26,541 --> 00:00:28,825
trimmed to just "Feed at Raffles".

8
00:00:28,825 --> 00:00:32,469
The second half pointed across
the Padang to the Raffles'

9
00:00:32,549 --> 00:00:36,846
great rival — a hotel that was,
for much of the nineteenth century,

10
00:00:36,926 --> 00:00:39,068
the grandest address in Singapore,

11
00:00:39,148 --> 00:00:42,857
and that today has vanished
so completely that most people

12
00:00:42,937 --> 00:00:46,400
walk over its site without
knowing it was ever there.

13
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,175
The site is easy to find.

14
00:00:49,175 --> 00:00:53,510
Stand in front of the former
Supreme Court building, now part of

15
00:00:53,590 --> 00:00:58,350
National Gallery Singapore, on Saint
Andrew's Road facing the Padang.

16
00:00:58,350 --> 00:01:01,655
The domed building that
houses the Gallery's Supreme

17
00:01:01,735 --> 00:01:05,105
Court wing stands where the
Hotel de l'Europe stood —

18
00:01:05,185 --> 00:01:09,140
the hotel's neighbour in its
lifetime, the Municipal Building,

19
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:12,475
is the Gallery's other
half, the former City Hall.

20
00:01:12,475 --> 00:01:15,916
The hotel faced the
Esplanade and the open green

21
00:01:15,996 --> 00:01:19,364
of the Padang, in the
company of Saint Andrew's

22
00:01:19,444 --> 00:01:22,525
Cathedral a short distance along the road.

23
00:01:22,525 --> 00:01:25,431
It was the best seat
in the civic district,

24
00:01:25,511 --> 00:01:28,775
and the hotel held it
for nearly seventy years.

25
00:01:28,775 --> 00:01:32,804
The Hotel de l'Europe did
not begin on that grand site,

26
00:01:32,884 --> 00:01:35,050
and it did not begin grandly.

27
00:01:35,050 --> 00:01:39,975
Its story on the Esplanade starts
with somebody else's hotel.

28
00:01:39,975 --> 00:01:43,520
In 1839 the French
showman Gaston Dutronquoy

29
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:48,298
established the London Hotel,
which by the mid-1840s stood

30
00:01:48,378 --> 00:01:52,250
at the corner of High
Street and the Esplanade.

31
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:55,602
Dutronquoy was a character
in his own right —

32
00:01:55,682 --> 00:01:59,949
among other ventures, he ran
Singapore's first advertised

33
00:02:00,029 --> 00:02:04,300
commercial photographic studio
out of the hotel in 1843.

34
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:09,435
After he left Singapore, a Madame
Esperanza took the place over in

35
00:02:09,515 --> 00:02:14,650
1858 and renamed it the Hotel de
l'Esperance — the Hotel of Hope.

36
00:02:14,650 --> 00:02:17,950
The Europe, meanwhile, began elsewhere.

37
00:02:17,950 --> 00:02:22,836
In 1857 another Frenchman, J.
Casteleyns, started a modest

38
00:02:22,916 --> 00:02:27,625
establishment called the
Hotel d'Europe on Hill Street.

39
00:02:27,625 --> 00:02:32,184
It moved to Beach Road in
1860, and in 1865 Casteleyns

40
00:02:32,264 --> 00:02:36,136
took over the Hotel de
l'Esperance premises on

41
00:02:36,216 --> 00:02:40,425
the Esplanade and gave
them his own hotel's name.

42
00:02:40,425 --> 00:02:44,325
The Hotel de l'Europe had
arrived on the Padang.

43
00:02:44,325 --> 00:02:48,619
For its first decades it was
comfortable rather than magnificent —

44
00:02:48,699 --> 00:02:50,739
about thirty rooms at the start,

45
00:02:50,819 --> 00:02:54,782
a cluster of buildings between
High Street and Coleman Street

46
00:02:54,862 --> 00:02:58,175
with separate quarters for
bachelors and families.

47
00:02:58,175 --> 00:03:01,946
A visitor in the early 1870s
found an American bar-room,

48
00:03:02,026 --> 00:03:06,004
a billiard room, and a reading
room stocked with newspapers

49
00:03:06,084 --> 00:03:09,717
and journals in five languages,
from New York, London,

50
00:03:09,797 --> 00:03:13,913
Calcutta, Shanghai and Yokohama
— a reminder that Singapore's

51
00:03:13,993 --> 00:03:17,775
travellers were already
citizens of a much wider world.

52
00:03:17,775 --> 00:03:22,271
Under proprietors C. A. Hjelmstrom
and A. Becker from 1871,

53
00:03:22,351 --> 00:03:26,614
and then Becker alone, the
hotel grew in standing until,

54
00:03:26,694 --> 00:03:30,414
by the 1880s, it was
described as the largest and

55
00:03:30,494 --> 00:03:35,225
best-known hotel in Singapore,
and one of the finest in Asia.

56
00:03:35,225 --> 00:03:39,823
The Raffles Hotel opened in
1887, and Singapore settled

57
00:03:39,903 --> 00:03:44,325
into a two-hotel town with
a clear social geography.

58
00:03:44,325 --> 00:03:49,228
A saying of the 1920s put it neatly:
tourists stayed at the Raffles,

59
00:03:49,308 --> 00:03:53,925
while European colonials stayed
at the Grand Hotel de l'Europe.

60
00:03:53,925 --> 00:03:56,935
The Raffles had the
glamour and the travellers;

61
00:03:57,015 --> 00:04:01,405
the Europe had the residents — the
merchants, officials and planters

62
00:04:01,485 --> 00:04:05,100
for whom it was less a hotel
than a club with bedrooms.

63
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:09,891
At some point in those years the
hotel was owned by the Alkaff family,

64
00:04:09,971 --> 00:04:14,136
the Hadhrami Arab merchants from
Tarim, in what is now Yemen,

65
00:04:14,216 --> 00:04:17,825
who had arrived in Singapore
in 1852 and built one of

66
00:04:17,905 --> 00:04:21,652
the town's great property
fortunes — the Alkaff Arcade,

67
00:04:21,732 --> 00:04:26,325
Alkaff Mansion and the Alkaff Lake
Gardens all carried their name.

68
00:04:26,325 --> 00:04:30,106
The National Archives records
that the Alkaffs owned

69
00:04:30,186 --> 00:04:34,562
the hotel at one time before
it was sold in the early 1900s,

70
00:04:34,642 --> 00:04:37,606
and the historian Maurizio Peleggi places

71
00:04:37,686 --> 00:04:41,325
their ownership before
the hotel's redevelopment.

72
00:04:41,325 --> 00:04:44,754
The records do not give
exact years, and the Alkaffs

73
00:04:44,834 --> 00:04:47,251
were investors rather than managers —

74
00:04:47,331 --> 00:04:51,436
the ground itself belonged to
the Catholic Portuguese Mission,

75
00:04:51,516 --> 00:04:55,025
which held the freehold
throughout the hotel's life.

76
00:04:55,025 --> 00:04:59,766
Ownership, lease and day-to-day
management were three different things,

77
00:04:59,846 --> 00:05:01,939
held by three different parties,

78
00:05:02,019 --> 00:05:06,025
which is part of why the hotel's
paper trail is so tangled.

79
00:05:06,025 --> 00:05:10,700
By the turn of the century the old
buildings were showing their age.

80
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:15,249
In 1904 the Straits Times called
the hotel a "barn-like erection

81
00:05:15,329 --> 00:05:20,312
jutting into the road-way", an eyesore
on the town's finest frontage —

82
00:05:20,392 --> 00:05:24,225
even if its bar and billiard
room still drew the eye.

83
00:05:24,225 --> 00:05:27,675
Something grander was clearly called for.

84
00:05:27,675 --> 00:05:31,550
The rebuild began as
someone else's project.

85
00:05:31,550 --> 00:05:36,983
In 1903 a syndicate formed by Sam
Tomlinson, a former municipal engineer,

86
00:05:37,063 --> 00:05:41,212
and Chye Tian Fook acquired
the hotel for redevelopment,

87
00:05:41,292 --> 00:05:44,687
and demolition and
rebuilding started in 1904,

88
00:05:44,767 --> 00:05:49,600
with the hotel partly occupied
while the work went on around it.

89
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:52,943
In November 1905 the
financing changed hands:

90
00:05:53,023 --> 00:05:57,584
Nissim Nassim Adis, one of
Singapore's richest self-made men,

91
00:05:57,664 --> 00:06:01,464
signed an agreement with
the builders and took over

92
00:06:01,544 --> 00:06:05,500
responsibility for the cost
of construction himself.

93
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:08,975
Adis's own story is worth a pause.

94
00:06:08,975 --> 00:06:13,872
Born in Howrah, India, in 1857,
the son of a Calcutta merchant,

95
00:06:13,952 --> 00:06:16,163
he was articled to attorneys,

96
00:06:16,243 --> 00:06:21,062
then went into exchange and
stockbroking — and lost a fortune,

97
00:06:21,142 --> 00:06:25,250
some 300,000 rupees, in a
Calcutta financial crisis.

98
00:06:25,250 --> 00:06:30,500
A second venture in Hong
Kong in 1888 crashed as well.

99
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:35,795
He arrived in Singapore in June 1893
with very little, and rebuilt everything:

100
00:06:35,875 --> 00:06:38,758
as head of Adis & Co
he made his fortune in

101
00:06:38,838 --> 00:06:43,719
stockbroking and real estate, built
himself Adis Lodge on Mount Sophia —

102
00:06:43,799 --> 00:06:47,785
Adis Road still carries his
name — and became the first Jew

103
00:06:47,865 --> 00:06:52,275
to serve as Master of the Lodge
of Saint Andrew in the Far East.

104
00:06:52,275 --> 00:06:55,195
The rebuilt hotel was so identified with

105
00:06:55,275 --> 00:06:58,575
him that people called
it the Adis Building.

106
00:06:58,575 --> 00:07:01,900
What he paid for was built to impress.

107
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:07,089
Part of the new hotel opened
in July 1906, and on the 7th of

108
00:07:07,169 --> 00:07:12,350
August 1907 Adis took possession
of the completed premises.

109
00:07:12,350 --> 00:07:15,856
The Grand Hotel de l'Europe,
as it now styled itself,

110
00:07:15,936 --> 00:07:21,606
was a Renaissance-style palace of nearly an
acre: two-storey-high Corinthian columns,

111
00:07:21,686 --> 00:07:24,650
detached pavilions for
dining and recreation,

112
00:07:24,730 --> 00:07:30,333
wide verandahs fitted with punkahs, and a
ground-floor colonnade of segmental arches

113
00:07:30,413 --> 00:07:34,675
between massive rusticated piers,
floored with encaustic tiles.

114
00:07:34,675 --> 00:07:38,431
Inside were 120 bedrooms,
each with its own balcony,

115
00:07:38,511 --> 00:07:43,006
dressing room and bathroom — a
standard of private comfort few

116
00:07:43,086 --> 00:07:46,399
Asian hotels could match
— lit by electricity,

117
00:07:46,479 --> 00:07:51,275
cooled by electric fans as well
as punkahs, and served by a lift.

118
00:07:51,275 --> 00:07:53,858
The hotel kept its own horse-drawn

119
00:07:53,938 --> 00:07:57,150
carriages to meet ships at Tanjong Pagar.

120
00:07:57,150 --> 00:08:01,500
Built and furnished, the whole
thing cost a million dollars.

121
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:05,896
Its dining room was the famous
room: nicknamed the "Bird Cage",

122
00:08:05,976 --> 00:08:09,875
about a thousand square feet
and almost free of columns,

123
00:08:09,955 --> 00:08:13,650
advertised as having "the
finest view east of Suez".

124
00:08:13,650 --> 00:08:15,573
There was a roof garden,

125
00:08:15,653 --> 00:08:19,325
and from 1920 a ladies'
lounge and tea room.

126
00:08:19,325 --> 00:08:22,094
Dinner was accompanied by serious music —

127
00:08:22,174 --> 00:08:26,541
the pianist Claude Oliveiro, who
played in the hotel's orchestra

128
00:08:26,621 --> 00:08:30,015
in its last years, remembered
programmes of opera,

129
00:08:30,095 --> 00:08:33,500
German waltzes and French
waltzes for the diners.

130
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:36,512
A daily rate in 1924, covering room,

131
00:08:36,592 --> 00:08:40,800
bath and food, started at
twelve Straits dollars.

132
00:08:40,800 --> 00:08:45,822
The Europe's most dramatic hour
came on the 15th of February 1915,

133
00:08:45,902 --> 00:08:50,850
when the sepoys of the 5th Light
Infantry mutinied in Singapore.

134
00:08:50,850 --> 00:08:54,216
With shooting in the
streets, the hotel doubled as

135
00:08:54,296 --> 00:08:57,731
a fortress and a sanctuary:
women and children took

136
00:08:57,811 --> 00:09:01,050
refuge inside it while
the revolt was put down.

137
00:09:01,050 --> 00:09:04,573
A few days later, crowds
were back on its verandahs,

138
00:09:04,653 --> 00:09:08,384
watching the parades on the
Padang — the hotel resuming

139
00:09:08,464 --> 00:09:12,275
its usual role as the grandstand
of the civic district.

140
00:09:12,275 --> 00:09:16,125
The end, when it came,
was slow and then sudden.

141
00:09:16,125 --> 00:09:20,251
Through the late 1920s the
hotel fell behind the times and

142
00:09:20,331 --> 00:09:24,747
into financial difficulty: the
plant was ageing, and the terms

143
00:09:24,827 --> 00:09:29,250
of its lease carried an obligation
to rebuild all over again.

144
00:09:29,250 --> 00:09:33,145
A redevelopment modelled on
Hong Kong's new Peninsula

145
00:09:33,225 --> 00:09:37,650
Hotel was planned and then
abandoned as the Depression bit.

146
00:09:37,650 --> 00:09:42,518
From 1913 the hotel had been
run by The Europe Hotel Ltd —

147
00:09:42,598 --> 00:09:47,296
the "Grand" was dropped from
its name by the late 1910s,

148
00:09:47,376 --> 00:09:51,300
though some records date
the renaming to 1918.

149
00:09:51,300 --> 00:09:55,192
In October 1932 the company
went into liquidation,

150
00:09:55,272 --> 00:10:00,038
and on the 30th of October 1932
the Straits Times carried the

151
00:10:00,118 --> 00:10:04,725
headline that closed the story:
"The Europe Hotel Closes."

152
00:10:04,725 --> 00:10:08,100
It had been dancing almost to the last.

153
00:10:08,100 --> 00:10:11,925
Five months earlier, in May
1932, the hotel was still

154
00:10:12,005 --> 00:10:16,346
advertising a special dinner
and dance every Saturday night,

155
00:10:16,426 --> 00:10:20,694
an orchestra playing light
music on the verandah and during

156
00:10:20,774 --> 00:10:24,900
meals every day, and an
orchestral concert every Sunday.

157
00:10:24,900 --> 00:10:28,423
The building stood empty
for a while as hopes of a

158
00:10:28,503 --> 00:10:32,250
new modern hotel on the
site were raised and failed.

159
00:10:32,250 --> 00:10:35,812
The Catholic Portuguese
Mission, the landowner,

160
00:10:35,892 --> 00:10:39,765
decided on demolition and
a block of service flats;

161
00:10:39,845 --> 00:10:43,100
the demolition was under way by late 1933.

162
00:10:43,100 --> 00:10:46,755
Then the government stepped
in, acquired the site —

163
00:10:46,835 --> 00:10:52,400
the purchase was completed in 1935 —
and chose it for the new Supreme Court.

164
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:56,777
The Supreme Court building,
designed by Frank Dorrington

165
00:10:56,857 --> 00:11:00,677
Ward and modelled in part
on London's Old Bailey,

166
00:11:00,757 --> 00:11:05,293
rose on the hotel's footprint:
foundation stone on the 1st

167
00:11:05,373 --> 00:11:09,750
of April 1937, declared open
on the 3rd of August 1939.

168
00:11:09,750 --> 00:11:14,771
It was the last great classical
building of colonial Singapore,

169
00:11:14,851 --> 00:11:17,767
home to the Supreme Court until 2005,

170
00:11:17,847 --> 00:11:23,435
a National Monument from 1992, and
since the 24th of November 2015 the

171
00:11:23,515 --> 00:11:28,050
former Supreme Court wing of
National Gallery Singapore.

172
00:11:28,050 --> 00:11:31,675
So the Europe never
really left the Padang.

173
00:11:31,675 --> 00:11:34,982
The traveller who follows
Kipling's advice today can

174
00:11:35,062 --> 00:11:39,020
still feed at Raffles — but to
sleep at the Hotel de l'Europe,

175
00:11:39,100 --> 00:11:41,755
you would have to sleep in an art gallery,

176
00:11:41,835 --> 00:11:45,728
on the exact ground where the
colonials took their dinners to

177
00:11:45,808 --> 00:11:49,325
the sound of waltzes, in the
finest view east of Suez.

178
00:11:49,325 --> 00:11:52,723
Where it fits in the bigger
story: For half a century

179
00:11:52,803 --> 00:11:56,332
the Hotel de l'Europe was
the other pole of Singapore's

180
00:11:56,412 --> 00:12:00,861
social world — the residents' hotel
against the travellers' Raffles —

181
00:12:00,941 --> 00:12:04,076
and its site has been a
seat of power ever since,

182
00:12:04,156 --> 00:12:07,175
first of hospitality,
then of law, now of art.

183
00:12:07,175 --> 00:12:12,743
Its million-dollar rebuild also belongs
to the generation of self-made fortunes,

184
00:12:12,823 --> 00:12:16,272
Adis's and the Alkaffs',
that built so much of the

185
00:12:16,352 --> 00:12:20,800
civic district and then vanished
with the world that made them.
